
==============================================================================
S1. Hypotenuse +- even leg are odd squares; (s,t)=(m+n,m-n) is the identity
==============================================================================
universe: all PPTs with c <= 1000; count = 158
PROVED and checked on the universe: c+b=(m+n)^2, c-b=(m-n)^2 (odd squares),
c+a=2m^2, c-a=2n^2 (never squares), a=st, b=(s^2-t^2)/2, c=(s^2+t^2)/2,
tan(phi/2)=n/m for phi opposite the even leg, tan(psi/2)=(m-n)/(m+n) for psi
opposite the odd leg.  Exactly ONE leg (the even one) satisfies the claim.
'half of their identity' made exact: for odd s>=3 the number of PPTs with
c+b=s^2 is phi(s)/2 (checked s=3..201, 100 values); s alone is one of
two coordinates, t=m-n is the other.  First fibres:
  s=3: c+b=9: [(3, 4, 5)]
  s=5: c+b=25: [(5, 12, 13), (15, 8, 17)]
  s=7: c+b=49: [(7, 24, 25), (21, 20, 29), (35, 12, 37)]
  s=9: c+b=81: [(9, 40, 41), (45, 28, 53), (63, 16, 65)]
  s=15: c+b=225: [(15, 112, 113), (105, 88, 137), (165, 52, 173), (195, 28, 197)]

==============================================================================
S2. Readings of 'hypotenuse = square + 1, altitude = square root'
==============================================================================
(b1) literal: hypotenuse k^2+1, altitude-to-hypotenuse sqrt(k).
    A right triangle with hypotenuse C and altitude H exists iff C >= 2H
    (legs are roots of X^2 - S X + C H with S^2 = C^2 + 2CH, (a-b)^2 = C^2-2CH).
    with u=sqrt(k): C-2H = u^4-2u+1 = (u - 1)*(u**3 + u**2 + u - 1)
    real root of u^3+u^2+u-1: u0 = 0.543689012692, k0 = u0^2 = 0.295597742522
    so a real right triangle exists iff k >= 1 or k <= k0; k=1 gives legs sqrt2, sqrt2, hyp 2.
    rational-leg instances need k=j^2 and both (j^4+1)(j^4+1+-2j) squares;
    j<=(2000): hits = [] (none).  j even: gcd(j^4+1,2j)=1 forces
    j^4+1 square, impossible (PROVED).  j odd: forces (j^4+1)/2 square, i.e.
    j^4+1=2w^2, equivalently a near-isosceles PPT whose leg sum is a square;
    squares among x^2-2y^2=-1 numerators through index 400: [(0, 1)]
    (only x=1, i.e. j=1, the isosceles sqrt2,sqrt2,2 case).  Beyond the finite
    range this is UNCITED-RECOLLECTION (Ljunggren-type quartic Pell result).
    3-4-5: hypotenuse 5 = 2^2+1 but altitude ab/c = 12/5, not sqrt(2);
    REFUTED as a description of 3-4-5 (minimal witness: the triple itself).
(b2) family (k^2-1, 2k, k^2+1) = Euclid (m,n)=(k,1):
    k= 2: (3, 4, 5)   primitive  altitude=12/5
    k= 3: (8, 6, 10)       gcd 2  altitude=24/5
    k= 4: (15, 8, 17)   primitive  altitude=120/17
    k= 5: (24, 10, 26)       gcd 2  altitude=120/13
    k= 6: (35, 12, 37)   primitive  altitude=420/37
    k= 7: (48, 14, 50)       gcd 2  altitude=336/25
    k= 8: (63, 16, 65)   primitive  altitude=1008/65
    k= 9: (80, 18, 82)       gcd 2  altitude=720/41
    k=10: (99, 20, 101)   primitive  altitude=1980/101
    k=11: (120, 22, 122)       gcd 2  altitude=1320/61
    hypotenuse = k^2+1 EXACTLY; primitive iff k even; first member k=2 is 3-4-5;
    the half-angle triangle (k,1,sqrt(k^2+1)) has leg k = sqrt(k^2): this is the
    only length equal to 'the square root of that same number'.  Angle opposite
    the even leg = 2*arctan(1/k) -> 0, vertex -> diameter endpoint.  HEURISTIC
    reading match: (b2) fits the words 'hypotenuse one greater than a square,
    3-4-5 first'; the 'altitude sqrt' clause fits only the half-angle leg k.
(b6) THM-3335 family: hypotenuse = (even square) + 1, even leg = that square:
    first members [(3, 4, 5), (17, 144, 145), (99, 4900, 4901), (577, 166464, 166465)]: (3,4,5) is first here too, but 'altitude sqrt'
    matches nothing in the triangle (sqrt(4)=2 is not a side or altitude).

==============================================================================
S3. Thales semicircle normalization and the (e/d, l, theta) chart
==============================================================================
Thales: the right-angle vertex lies on the circle with the hypotenuse as
DIAMETER (radius 1/2 after normalization), not as radius; the central angle
is 2*theta (inscribed-angle theorem).  With a<b, theta=arcsin(a/c) in (0,pi/4).
e=a^2/c^2=sin^2, d=b^2/c^2=cos^2, l=ab/c^2=sin(2theta)/2, e/d=tan^2 theta.
  d/dtheta (e/d) = 2*tan(theta)/cos(theta)**2  > 0 on (0,pi/2)
  d/dtheta l     = cos(2*theta)   > 0 on (0,pi/4)
  limits at theta=pi/4: (e/d, l) = (1, 1/2); isosceles endpoint is not a PPT
  PROVED: (e/d, l, theta) is a monotone one-parameter chart in theta.
  FINITE-EXACT on 158 PPTs with c<=1000: e+d=1, l^2=ed, e/d=(a/b)^2,
  vertex ((d-e)/2, l) = (1/2)*(b^2-a^2, 2ab)/c^2 = half-scaled ANGLE-DOUBLED triple,
  and tan(theta/2) is n/m (even shorter leg) or (m-n)/(m+n) (odd shorter leg).
  Parity refinement: tan(theta/2)=p/q reduced with p,q opposite parity <-> theta
  opposite the even leg; p,q both odd <-> theta opposite the odd leg.  Both occur.
  Lost-information witness: legs (1,2) give rational e/d=1/4, l=2/5 but hypotenuse
  sqrt5; (e/d,l) depend on tan(theta) only; PPT <=> tan(theta/2) also rational.
Extremal family A (theta->0): (k^2-1,2k,k^2+1), k even, and (s,(s^2-1)/2,(s^2+1)/2), s odd.
Extremal family B (theta->pi/4): near-isosceles |a-b|=1, Pell (m,n)->(2m+n,m):
    (a,b,c)=(3,4,5), (m,n)=(2,1), (s,t)=(3,1), tan(theta/2)=1/3
    (a,b,c)=(21,20,29), (m,n)=(5,2), (s,t)=(7,3), tan(theta/2)=3/7
    (a,b,c)=(119,120,169), (m,n)=(12,5), (s,t)=(17,7), tan(theta/2)=7/17
    (a,b,c)=(697,696,985), (m,n)=(29,12), (s,t)=(41,17), tan(theta/2)=17/41
    (a,b,c)=(4059,4060,5741), (m,n)=(70,29), (s,t)=(99,41), tan(theta/2)=41/99
    (a,b,c)=(23661,23660,33461), (m,n)=(169,70), (s,t)=(239,99), tan(theta/2)=99/239
    (a,b,c)=(137903,137904,195025), (m,n)=(408,169), (s,t)=(577,239), tan(theta/2)=239/577
    (a,b,c)=(803761,803760,1136689), (m,n)=(985,408), (s,t)=(1393,577), tan(theta/2)=577/1393
    PROVED: |a-b|=1 <=> (m-n)^2-2n^2=+-1 (Pell); c_{i+1}=6c_i-c_{i-1}, x_{i+1}=6x_i-x_{i-1}+2,
    tan(theta/2)=t/s runs through the convergents of sqrt2-1.
    FINITE-EXACT: near-isosceles hypotenuses c<=10^6 are exactly [5, 29, 169, 985, 5741, 33461, 195025].
29 test against THM-4146 (D=a^2+2b^2-ab, cycle -(a+b)->h->-(b-a)):
    (3,4,5): D=29, orbit ['-7', '5', '-1', '-7']  (closes, THM-4146 (33))
    (20,21,29): D=862, orbit ['-41', '39', '659/21', '54139/9261']  (does NOT close)
    D(m,n) = m**4 - 2*m**3*n + 6*m**2*n**2 + 2*m*n**3 + n**4
    c_next(m,n) = 5*m**2 + 4*m*n + n**2
    D - c_next = m**4 - 2*m**3*n + 6*m**2*n**2 - 5*m**2 + 2*m*n**3 - 4*m*n + n**4 - n**2
    integer agreement with 300>=m>n>=1: [(2, 1)]
    REFUTED as a map: 29 is 5^2+2^2 in THM-4146 (c^2 + b(b-a), b(b-a)=4 at 3-4-5)
    and c=m^2+n^2 for the Pell pair (5,2); the two polynomials in (m,n) agree only
    at (2,1).  (20,21,29) is not in the 3:4:5 class and plays no role in the cycle.

==============================================================================
S4. Angle doubling = Gaussian squaring; hypotenuse squaring forest; x^2-2
==============================================================================
PROVED: cos(2theta)=2cos^2-1; with y=2cos(theta), y -> y^2-2 (Chebyshev T_2).
PROVED: (b+ai)^2 = (b^2-a^2) + 2ab i, |.|=c^2: the doubling (a,b,c)->(2ab,|b^2-a^2|,c^2)
is Gaussian squaring; THM-3333's lift Phi(m,n) = (m+ni)^2 is the SAME map, so the
half-angle (m,n) triangle -> PPT is one doubling step.
universe: PPTs with c<=10000: 1593
PPTs with square hypotenuse, c<=10000: 16
  (7,24,25) = double of (3, 4, 5)   (m,n)=(4,3), sqrt(c)=5
  (119,120,169) = double of (5, 12, 13)   (m,n)=(12,5), sqrt(c)=13
  (161,240,289) = double of (15, 8, 17)   (m,n)=(15,8), sqrt(c)=17
  (41,840,841) = double of (21, 20, 29)   (m,n)=(21,20), sqrt(c)=29
  (527,336,625) = double of (7, 24, 25)   (m,n)=(24,7), sqrt(c)=25
  (1081,840,1369) = double of (35, 12, 37)   (m,n)=(35,12), sqrt(c)=37
  (1519,720,1681) = double of (9, 40, 41)   (m,n)=(40,9), sqrt(c)=41
  (1241,2520,2809) = double of (45, 28, 53)   (m,n)=(45,28), sqrt(c)=53
  (721,5280,5329) = double of (55, 48, 73)   (m,n)=(55,48), sqrt(c)=73
  (2047,3696,4225) = double of (33, 56, 65)   (m,n)=(56,33), sqrt(c)=65
  (3713,2016,4225) = double of (63, 16, 65)   (m,n)=(63,16), sqrt(c)=65
  (3479,1320,3721) = double of (11, 60, 61)   (m,n)=(60,11), sqrt(c)=61
  (959,9360,9409) = double of (65, 72, 97)   (m,n)=(72,65), sqrt(c)=97
  (4633,5544,7225) = double of (77, 36, 85)   (m,n)=(77,36), sqrt(c)=85
  (6887,2184,7225) = double of (13, 84, 85)   (m,n)=(84,13), sqrt(c)=85
  (4879,6240,7921) = double of (39, 80, 89)   (m,n)=(80,39), sqrt(c)=89
PROVED: (a,b,c) is an angle-double of a PPT <=> c is a perfect square <=> (n,m,sqrt c)
is a PPT; doubling is a bijection PPT(c) -> PPT(c^2), both of size 2^(omega(c)-1).
FINITE-EXACT: #PPT(c<=100) = 16 = #square-hypotenuse PPTs (c<=10000).
Hypotenuse orbit c, c^2, c^4, ... is a ray of the squaring forest of
arithmetic_braids_20260917_summand.md section 2; on hypotenuses the square-root
ancestry lifts bijectively to PPT halving (the summand note's hostile concerned
arbitrary integers, not hypotenuses).
  doubling chain from 3-4-5: (3, 4, 5) -> (24, 7, 25) -> (336, 527, 625) -> (354144, 164833, 390625) -> ...
PROVED: |y|>=3 => |y^2-2| > |y| (escape); odd-prime denominators double their
valuation and 2-adic ones too, so PrePer(y^2-2, Q) = {-2,-1,0,1,2}.  Graph:
   2 (2cos     0) ->  2
  -2 (2cos    pi) ->  2
   0 (2cos  pi/2) -> -2
   1 (2cos  pi/3) -> -1
  -1 (2cos 2pi/3) -> -1
  = angle doubling on {0, pi/3, pi/2, 2pi/3, pi}: pi->0, pi/2->pi, pi/3->2pi/3->4pi/3~2pi/3.
  N=9: ord_9(2)=6; 2cos(2pi k/9) (min poly y**3 - 3*y + 1) has exact period 3.
  N=7: ord_7(2)=3; 2cos(2pi k/7) (min poly y**3 + y**2 - 2*y - 1) has exact period 3.
  N=9: the angle orbit {1,2,4,8,7,5} has order 6 = ord_9(2) (the same fact as the
  inherited 'exponents 1,5,3 mod 6' row law); its fold by -1 is the 3-cycle {1,2,4}.
  The Collatz row residues {2,5,8} mod 9 meet each +-pair {1,8},{2,7},{4,5} exactly once
  (PROVED trivially: -1 swaps the classes 1,2 mod 3).  Order-six-over-three via a
  central -1 is the shape shared with THM-4139's B^3=-I; no map between the two
  3-cycles exists (cubic irrationals versus quarter-integers).

